Files taken from CookCLI
------------------------

The visual design of CookLangHub is the visual design of CookCLI, so that
the Cooklang ecosystem reads as one family. These files are copies or
close adaptations:

    tailwind.config.js           copied
    static/css/input.css         copied, then extended
    static/css/custom-styles.css copied
    static/css/cooking-mode.css  copied
    static/css/theme.src.css     copied from the inline style of their
                                 base template, then corrected in two
                                 rules: CookCLI writes template syntax
                                 inside those two selectors, which their
                                 template expands and a plain stylesheet
                                 cannot, so the addresses are now the
                                 addresses of this application
    static/js/cooking-mode.js    copied, with four changes, each one
                                 recorded in the header of the file:
                                 CookCLI starts Cook mode from an
                                 `onclick` attribute, which the Content
                                 Security Policy here does not allow, so
                                 the button is bound in the file instead;
                                 the focus stays inside the dialog; a
                                 card that is not on the screen is
                                 `inert`; and an ingredient to gather is
                                 a checkbox, so a cook with a keyboard
                                 can tick one off
    static/js/src/cooklang-mode.js
                                 copied, then corrected: the mode gave
                                 CodeMirror its own words for a token
                                 rather than a tag CodeMirror knows

    templates/*.html             the class names, the markup shape, and
                                 the component structure follow CookCLI

The Recipe editor is the other high-value reuse. CookCLI wrote the
CodeMirror 6 Cooklang mode, and this project uses it:

    static/js/src/cooklang-mode.js  copied
    static/js/src/editor.js         adapted
    static/js/editor.js             GENERATED from the two files above by
                                    scripts/build-editor.mjs

Each adapted file carries the CookCLI copyright notice in its own header.

static/js/editor.js also carries CodeMirror 6, which is copyright Marijn
Haverbeke and others and is licensed under the MIT License.


Changes made
------------

CookLangHub is a different product with a different backend, so the
templates carry different data and different actions. The design language,
the palette, the component classes, and the layout come from CookCLI
unchanged.

The editor differs in four places, and each one is recorded in the header of
the file. CookCLI reads Cooklang messages and completions from a language
server, which this project does not have. The preview here is
server-rendered, because this project has exactly one Cooklang parser and a
second parser in the browser would drift from it. The palette of the editor
comes from the class that the server wrote on the page, to match how the
rest of CookLangHub picks light or dark. And the editor lives in a shadow
root, so that the CSS of CodeMirror is adopted rather than put inside the
page, which keeps the Content Security Policy at `default-src 'self'`.


axe-core
--------

The accessibility check uses axe-core, which is copyright Deque Systems,
Inc. and is licensed under the Mozilla Public License, version 2.0.

    scripts/vendor/axe.min.js           copied, unchanged
    scripts/vendor/LICENSE-MPL-axe-core the full MPL-2.0 text

The file is in this repository and not on another host, because the check
must run with no network and the policy of this application is
`default-src 'self'`. It is a development tool. The runtime is the Rust
binary plus the files in `static/`, and axe-core is in neither.

The MPL-2.0 is a licence on the file. It does not reach the rest of this
work, which stays under the AGPL-3.0-or-later.
